---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/base';
---
<Guide
  title="Getting told about new enquiries"
  heading="Getting told about"
  thin="new enquiries."
  intro="When somebody fills in the form on your website, that is a customer with their hand up. This makes sure an email lands in your inbox the moment it happens, instead of it sitting on a screen you forgot to check."
  description="How to turn on email notifications so you hear about new enquiries from your website straight away."
>
  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>This is already switched on.</strong> You do not have to do anything to start getting these. The two minutes here are worth it anyway — to see where the setting lives, and to send them somewhere you actually read.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="steps">
    <div class="step reveal">
      <h2>Open your profile</h2>
      <p>Sign in at <a href="https://my.weaverdigital.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">my.weaverdigital.io</a>, then find the menu with your name on it and choose <strong>Profile</strong>. Everything on this page is about you rather than your website.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="step reveal">
      <h2>Check the notification switch</h2>
      <p>Look for <strong>Email notifications</strong>. There is a switch for each kind of message your site can receive — if your site asks people for estimate requests, that is the one you will see.</p>
      <p>It should already be on. If it is not, flick it on. The change saves itself.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="step reveal">
      <h2>Send them where you will see them</h2>
      <p>By default these go to the same address you sign in with. If you would rather they went somewhere else — the inbox you actually have on your phone, or an address your office manager watches — put that address in the field for it on the same page.</p>
      <p>Changing it here does not change how you sign in. That stays the same.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="step reveal">
      <h2>Test it on yourself</h2>
      <p>Worth doing once, so you know what you are looking for at seven in the morning:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Open your own website and fill in the form as if you were a customer.</li>
        <li>Put something obvious in the message like <code>test, ignore</code>.</li>
        <li>Check your email. It should turn up within a minute or two.</li>
        <li>Sign in and you will find the same thing waiting in your enquiries.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note warn">
        <p>If the first one lands in spam, open it and mark it <strong>not spam</strong>. Do that once and the rest will come through properly — and these are the emails you least want to miss.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <h2>What the email tells you</h2>
    <p>You get who it is, how to reach them, and what they said — enough to call them straight back without signing in to anything. Signing in is for when you want to keep track of where each one got to.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="guide-section">
    <h2>Keeping track of them</h2>
    <p>Every enquiry has a status so you can see what you have dealt with and what is still owed an answer. Move one along as you go and the list stays honest. Anything still waiting on you shows up on your dashboard the moment you sign in.</p>
    <p>Not sure how to get in? Start with <a href={url('/guides/signing-in')}>signing in to your website</a>.</p>
  </div>
</Guide>
